course: dictionary
lesson: 2222
title: A word for family far and near
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A cousin is a person, so it is a noun. You can find its meaning by reading the words around it. In «At his cousin's birthday they meet for real», a birthday party with family around suggests a cousin is someone from your family, not a stranger.
explain: Read the clues in «his cousin heard» from the rumour sentence. Someone heard the news from a cousin, so the cousin is a person who could pass on news to you. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: The dictionary says a cousin is the child of your aunt or uncle, and a first cousin shares your grandparents. Try it on «His cousins from California talk about private schools». Cousins from California are family members who live far away, so the meaning fits.
fact: A cousin is the child of your aunt or uncle.
fact: A first cousin shares your grandparents.
fact: «Cousin» is a noun, and its plural is «cousins».
fact: Cousins can live close by or far away, such as a cousin in Sydney and you in Leeds.
fact: Context clues, such as a birthday party or a family visit, help you understand who a cousin is.
exercise: «At his cousin's birthday they meet for real and nobody knows what to say.» Who is a cousin? | A teacher at the school | A friend from school | *The child of your aunt or uncle | A grandparent
exercise: «At Thanksgiving his cousins from California talk about private schools.» Who are Josiah's cousins? | *Children of his aunt or uncle | His classmates | His teachers | Strangers from the next street
exercise: Sam and his cousin share the same grandparents. What kind of cousin is this? | A neighbour | *A first cousin | A stranger | A school friend
test: «A school legend is usually told about "a kid at my cousin's school".» Why might this story be made up? | The cousin is a teacher | It is about a famous film | It is written in a book | *It is a friend-of-a-friend story, and you cannot ask that person
test: Picture a cousin in Sydney and you in Leeds. What does «cousin» mean here? | A teacher from another school | A grandparent who lives in your house | A person in your class | *A family member, the child of your aunt or uncle, who lives far away
exam: Which is the best meaning of cousin? | *The child of your aunt or uncle | A child in your class at school | Your grandparent | A person who lives in your house
